The attached image is an immunoblot (Figure 7D) illustrating the production and secretion of interleukin-1 beta (IL-1β) by bone marrow-derived dendritic cells (BMDCs) under different stimulation conditions. It demonstrates that when BMDCs are primed with lipopolysaccharide (LPS) and stimulated with Nano-11, the active 17 kD form of IL-1β is secreted into the supernatant. In contrast, the inactive 30 kD pro-IL-1β is observed in cell lysates, particularly when LPS is present, indicating that Nano-11 promotes the cleavage of pro-IL-1β into its active form, a critical step in immune activation.